                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_capbreach:        `<strong>The CFO kills it before the next cold snap.</strong> Your plan blew the €2.0M cap and dragged contribution margin below the 30% floor — lovely service, unaffordable. Which parts of personalisation can the <em>interaction design</em> carry instead of more spend?`,
                    coach_r1_touchpoint_right: `<strong>That's where value collapses.</strong> Onboarding and coaching are exactly where the diagnostics point — 62% never finished onboarding, 28% found coaching preachy. Now name whose resources failed at that touchpoint.`,
                    coach_r1_acquire:       `<strong>Careful — that's the marketing reflex.</strong> The ad may over-promise, but the value didn't collapse in the funnel; it collapsed in the interaction after sign-up. Look at onboarding and coaching, where the complaints actually cluster.`,
                    coach_r1_renewal:       `<strong>“They leave over price” is the comfortable answer.</strong> A retention discount buys a month and burns margin. Customers aren't leaving over price — the interaction destroyed the value you promised. Where did value collapse, upstream of renewal?`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You located the co-destruction at the right touchpoint AND named it as a failure of the <em>integration</em> — the firm asked for resources the customer never gave, in a flow that misread the household. That is exactly the lens that tells you what to redesign.`,
                    coach_r1_oneside:       `<strong>You've blamed one side only.</strong> Co-destruction is rarely all the firm's fault or all the customer's — it lives in the integration between them. A confusing app AND a customer who never shared occupancy data both have to be designed for.`,
                    coach_r1_neither:       `<strong>That's how you get the same backlash again.</strong> Calling it “normal churn, nothing to design” is precisely the management instinct that treats churn as a marketing metric. It is an interaction problem — there is a touchpoint to fix.`,
                    coach_r1_marketing:     `<strong>You're framing this as marketing.</strong> Low willingness to treat churn as interaction design means you'll reach for a discount or a campaign. The value collapsed in the interaction — a discount fixes none of that.`,

                    coach_r2_light:         `<strong>Light-touch integration is the smart trade.</strong> A guided wizard that captures 80% of the occupancy data with 20% of the friction gets you clean enough data without the activation drop-off that mandatory schedules create.`,
                    coach_r2_friction:      `<strong>You traded one co-destruction point for another.</strong> Requiring a full schedule before activation guarantees clean data — and a ~30% activation drop-off. Customers who never start are co-destruction too. Where's the lighter touch that gets 80% of the data with 20% of the friction?`,
                    coach_r2_noonboard:     `<strong>Leaving onboarding broken leaves 62% of the failure in place.</strong> Onboarding is the touchpoint where most value was co-destroyed. Doing nothing here means the next cold snap hits the same un-engaged households.`,
                    coach_r2_redesign:      `<strong>This is redesign, not addition.</strong> A light-touch wizard plus an honest “comfort vs savings” reset fixes the two worst touchpoints by <em>simplifying</em> the interaction — the opposite of piling on features. You cut backlash without blowing the cap.`,
                    coach_r2_morefeatures:  `<strong>More features is more surface area for co-destruction.</strong> Your diagnostic says 62% never finished onboarding — you're adding to an interaction that is already overloaded, and a louder savings claim re-opens the very over-promise that caused the backlash. What did you <em>simplify</em>, not just add?`,
                    coach_r2_silent:        `<strong>Silence keeps the over-promise alive.</strong> If you don't reset the “comfort vs savings” expectation, the next cold snap produces the same gap between promise and bill — and the same backlash.`,
                    coach_r2_agents:        `<strong>Sixty coaches is lovely service the CFO will kill.</strong> Hiring humans to personally guide every household costs €1.6M and crowds out the rest of the redesign under the €2.0M cap. Which parts of personalisation can the interaction design carry instead of a human?`,
                    coach_r2_generic:       `<strong>Generic scripts keep the “preachy” problem.</strong> 28% already rated the nudges irrelevant. The same script for everyone is the co-destruction at the coaching touchpoint — relevance comes from real usage, not a louder script.`,
                    coach_r2_uncurated:     `<strong>An un-curated community invites misuse.</strong> A peer community is cheap co-creation at scale, but with almost no moderation budget it becomes a place where the savings-guarantee trick spreads. Curate it or don't open it.`,

                    coach_r3_mutual:        `<strong>A mutual, easy-to-honour rule is the spine of co-creation.</strong> The firm provides coaching and tools, the customer shares a simple schedule, and the guarantee rests on verified behaviour. Crucially you made the customer's part easy — a contribution, not a barrier.`,
                    coach_r3_firmheavy:     `<strong>“We'll deliver savings regardless” re-opens the over-promise.</strong> If the firm guarantees the outcome without the customer integrating their resources, you've recreated the exact gap that caused the cold-snap backlash. Value is co-created — the customer has a part.`,
                    coach_r3_customerheavy: `<strong>Heavy obligations are a friction barrier.</strong> Demanding paperwork and proof before any guarantee turns the customer's contribution into a wall. That's the mandatory-onboarding mistake wearing a contract — it co-destroys value by making integration too costly.`,
                    coach_r3_norule:        `<strong>No explicit rule is today's problem.</strong> The implicit contract is exactly what let expectations drift and the guarantee get gamed. A co-creation rule has to name who contributes what, or every touchpoint stays ambiguous.`,
                    coach_r3_designout:     `<strong>Designed-out risk beats policing.</strong> Capping the trigger, detecting anomalous schedules and reframing the guarantee around verified behaviour makes the trick simply stop working — and you didn't have to treat 240,000 customers like fraudsters to do it.`,
                    coach_r3_punitive:      `<strong>You punished 240,000 for the 4,000.</strong> You recovered €88,000 and signalled to your most engaged customers that you see them as fraudsters. Co-destruction now spreads to the loyal base. Could you have designed the trigger so the trick just stops working — without the blanket enforcement?`,
                    coach_r3_ignore:        `<strong>Ignoring it lets the trick spread.</strong> €88k is small today, but it's rising as the hack spreads on the forums — and an un-policed, un-redesigned guarantee erodes the honest majority's trust in the whole mechanism. Design it out.`,
                    coach_r3_underfunded:   `<strong>You chose to design it out but under-funded the build.</strong> Detection and trigger-redesign need real engineering effort; starve it and the anomalous schedules slip through anyway. Fund the fix or it isn't a fix.`,
                    coach_r3_lowsignal:     `<strong>You chased the €88k over the signal to the base.</strong> Recovering a small sum while telling 236,000 loyal customers you don't trust them is a bad trade. The reputational cost of treating loyal customers like fraudsters dwarfs €88k.`,

                    coach_r4_reengage:      `<strong>Re-engaging mid-onboarding households first is the right sequence.</strong> The cold snap lands in 45 days; the customers most exposed are the ones still un-integrated. Finish their onboarding before the cold hits and you protect the base from a second backlash.`,
                    coach_r4_acquire:       `<strong>Acquiring over a leaky journey just churns faster.</strong> Replacing churned subscribers before you've fixed the interaction pours new customers into the same co-destruction. Re-engage the at-risk base first; acquire once the journey holds.`,
                    coach_r4_freeze:        `<strong>Freezing until the board review is the worst option.</strong> The cold snap doesn't wait for your governance calendar. Households mid-onboarding when the cold hits will spike bills again — and the press narrative becomes permanent.`,
                    coach_r4_removeall:     `<strong>Removing the schedule entirely re-opens the gap.</strong> Activation soars, but you're back to un-integrated households whose bills spike in the cold — the exact co-destruction you just closed. Ease the friction, don't delete the integration.`,
                    coach_r4_forceharder:   `<strong>Forcing the full schedule harder makes the drop-off worse.</strong> The wizard already cut activation; cranking the friction up trades even more activations away. Progressive onboarding gets the core data without the wall.`,
                    coach_r4_board_strong:  `<strong>That's a board-ready case.</strong> You re-engaged the at-risk households first, eased friction without re-opening the gap, and defended redesign over a discount or scrapping the guarantee — with leading indicators the board can actually watch. That survives the next cold snap.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>A blanket discount buys a month and burns margin.</strong> The CMO will love it and it fixes nothing — the value collapsed in the interaction, and a discount doesn't repair a single touchpoint. You'll be back here after the next cold snap, poorer.`,
                    coach_r4_scrap:         `<strong>Scrapping the guarantee throws out the mechanism to kill the misuse.</strong> The CFO gets their wish, but you've removed a feature loyal customers value to stop 4,000 gamers — when you could have designed the trigger so the trick just stops working. That's punishing everyone again.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
